      "body": "A pedigree is a family tree that tracks one trait through generations.\n\nSymbols:\n- A square is a male. A circle is a female.\n- A shaded shape has the trait. An unshaded shape does not. A half-shaded shape (used in some pedigrees) is a known carrier.\n- A horizontal line between a square and a circle joins two parents. A vertical line drops from that line to their children, who are joined by a horizontal line above them, oldest on the left.\n- Generations are numbered with Roman numerals (I, II, III), and people in a generation with numbers (II-1, II-2).\n\nClues to the pattern:\n- Two unaffected parents with an affected child: the trait is recessive. Both parents must be carriers.\n- An affected child always has at least one affected parent, in every generation: the trait is probably dominant.\n- Many more affected males than females, passed from carrier mothers to sons: the trait is probably X-linked recessive.\n\nNot every trait is simply dominant or recessive. In incomplete dominance, the heterozygote is in between (pink from red and white). In codominance, both alleles show fully (red hairs and white hairs). Some genes have more than two alleles in the population, like the ABO blood group gene, even though each person carries only two.",
      "id": "pedigree-guide",
      "title": "How to Read a Pedigree"
    }
  ],
